@node LibreClaw Scripting
@section Scripting via cmacs-gi

Libreclaw 0.18.0 ships @file{Lc-1.0.gir}/@file{.typelib} as part of
the default build, so cmacs-gi's auto-wiring can reach every
libreclaw public API from Elisp without handwritten wrappers.

@subsection Loading the typelib

@example
(gi-require "Lc" "1.0")
@end example

@code{cmacs-libreclaw-start} does this automatically when cmacs-gi
is loaded.

@subsection Getting the app handle

@example
(let ((app (cmacs-libreclaw-get-app)))
  (gi-method app "get_channel_manager"))
@end example

@subsection Connecting to signals

@example
(let* ((app (cmacs-libreclaw-get-app))
       (mgr (gi-method app "get_channel_manager")))
  (gobject-connect mgr "message-received"
    (lambda (ch msg)
      (message "Got %s from %s"
               (gi-method msg "get_body")
               (gi-method msg "get_sender_name")))))
@end example

The signal callback runs on the Emacs main thread via cmacs's
dispatch guard — no threading concerns.

@subsection Scripting the hatch API

@example
(let ((ctx (gi-call "Lc" "HatchContext_new" "/tmp/scripted-ws")))
  (gi-method ctx "set_workspace_name" "scripted")
  (gi-method ctx "set_ai_provider" 'claude)
  (gi-method ctx "add_local_channel" "scripted> ")
  (gi-method ctx "finalize" nil))
@end example

The DEFUN wrappers @code{cmacs-libreclaw-hatch-*} are usually
preferable because they do argument validation and error
translation, but the direct GI path is available.
